 "drivingQuestion": "May hot water and oil be placed directly on a wound on Shabbat?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "objections to Rav are deflected; Shmuel gets explicit support; no ruling",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.134b.18",
   "daf": "134b",
   "sentenceId": 17,
   "he": "גּוּפָא. אָמַר רַב: אֵין מוֹנְעִין חַמִּין וָשֶׁמֶן מֵעַל גַּבֵּי מַכָּה בְּשַׁבָּת. וּשְׁמוּאֵל אָמַר: נוֹתֵן חוּץ לַמַּכָּה, וְשׁוֹתֵת וְיוֹרֵד לַמַּכָּה.",
   "en": "Rav’s statement with regard to the treatment of a wound on Shabbat was mentioned in passing. The Gemara proceeds to discuss the «matter itself. Rav said: One does not prevent» placing «hot water and oil on a wound on Shabbat. And Shmuel said:» It is prohibited to place hot water or oil directly onto the wound, because it appears to be medical treatment. Rather, «one places» the hot water or the oil «outside the wound,» above it, «and it flows and descends to the wound» itself."
  },
  {
   "id": "shabbat.134b.19",
   "daf": "134b",
   "sentenceId": 18,
   "he": "מֵיתִיבִי: אֵין נוֹתְנִין שֶׁמֶן וְחַמִּין עַל גַּבֵּי מוֹךְ לִיתֵּן עַל גַּבֵּי מַכָּה בַּשַּׁבָּת! הָתָם מִשּׁוּם סְחִיטָה.",
   "en": "The Gemara «raises an objection» from that which was taught in a baraita: «One may not place oil or hot water on soft material to place» it «on a wound on Shabbat.» This supports the opinion of Shmuel, who prohibits performing actions that appear to be medical treatment. However, that ruling contradicts the opinion of Rav. The Gemara answers: «There,» the baraita prohibited placing oil or hot water on a rag, not because it appears to be medical treatment; «rather,» it is prohibited «due to» concern that one might perform the prohibited labor of «squeezing.»"
  },
  {
   "id": "shabbat.134b.20",
   "daf": "134b",
   "sentenceId": 19,
   "he": "תָּא שְׁמַע: אֵין נוֹתְנִין חַמִּין וָשֶׁמֶן עַל גַּבֵּי מוֹךְ, שֶׁעַל גַּבֵּי מַכָּה בַּשַּׁבָּת. הָתָם נָמֵי מִשּׁוּם סְחִיטָה.",
   "en": "The Gemara cites an additional proof. «Come» and «hear» that which was taught in a similar baraita: «One may not place oil or hot water on soft material that is on a wound on Shabbat.» This baraita supports the opinion of Shmuel. The Gemara answers: «There too,» it is prohibited «due to» concern for «squeezing.»"
  },
  {
   "id": "shabbat.134b.21",
   "daf": "134b",
   "sentenceId": 20,
   "he": "תַּנְיָא כְּווֹתֵיהּ דִּשְׁמוּאֵל: אֵין נוֹתְנִין חַמִּין וָשֶׁמֶן עַל גַּבֵּי מַכָּה בַּשַּׁבָּת, אֲבָל נוֹתְנִין חוּץ לַמַּכָּה וְשׁוֹתֵת וְיוֹרֵד לַמַּכָּה.",
   "en": "A baraita «was taught» explicitly «in accordance with» the opinion of «Shmuel: One may not place oil or hot water on a wound on Shabbat; however, one may place it outside the wound so it flows and descends to the wound.»"
  },
  {
   "id": "shabbat.134b.22",
   "daf": "134b",
   "sentenceId": 21,
   "he": "תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: נוֹתְנִין עַל גַּבֵּי הַמַּכָּה מוֹךְ יָבֵשׁ וּסְפוֹג יָבֵשׁ, אֲבָל לֹא גֶּמִי יָבֵשׁ וְלֹא כְּתִיתִין יְבֵשִׁין. קַשְׁיָא כְּתִיתִין אַכְּתִיתִין! לָא קַשְׁיָא: הָא בְּחַדְתֵי, הָא בְּעַתִּיקֵי. אָמַר אַבָּיֵי: שְׁמַע מִינַּהּ הָנֵי כְּתִיתִין מַסּוּ.",
   "en": "«The Sages taught» another baraita: «One may place a dry compress and a dry sponge on a wound, but not a dry reed and not dry rags.» The Gemara comments: This is «difficult,» as the first statement with regard to «rags,» i.e., a dry compress which is a type of rag, contradicts the second statement with regard to «rags,» as the baraita first permitted placing rags on a wound and then prohibited doing so. The Gemara answers: This is «not difficult. This» statement, which prohibits placing rags on a wound, is referring «to new» rags, which heal the wound; «that» statement, which permits placing rags on a wound, is referring «to old» rags, which are not effective in healing. «Abaye said: Conclude from this» that «these rags heal,» which is useful information with regard to the treatment of wounds."
  }
 ],
